<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of São Mamede</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1128">1128</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of São Mamede was fought on 24 June 1128 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guimaraes">Guimarães</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> and the forces of his mother, Countess Teresa, and her Galician ally Fernão Peres de Trava. Afonso's victory gave him control of the County of Portugal, and it is traditionally seen as the founding event of Portuguese independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sao-mamede.jpg" alt="Battle of São Mamede" width="320" height="126" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acácio Lino (1878–1956) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalha_de_S%C3%A3o_Mamede_(1922).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1096 King Alfonso VI of León granted the County of Portugal to Henry, a brother of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, who married Alfonso's daughter Theresa. After Henry's death in 1112 Theresa ruled as regent and, from 1117, signed as queen, seeking independence from León and from her sister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile">Queen Urraca</a>. Her involvement in the quarrels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> made the Galician noble Fernão Peres de Trava her close associate and, by 1123, her equal in power; clerical writers called the relationship adulterous, and the pair had a daughter.</p><p>Disgruntled Portuguese nobles gathered around her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a>, among them Archbishop Paio Mendes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> and the Moniz brothers of Ribadouro. In 1125 his tutor Egas Moniz took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> to be knighted by his cousin Alfonso VII.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In September 1127 Alfonso VII, now king of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, invaded Portugal to force Theresa and Afonso to acknowledge him as overlord. During the siege of Guimarães Egas Moniz won his withdrawal by standing surety for his pupil's loyalty. The six-week invasion reduced Portugal to a province and cost the lands north of the Minho, and Theresa and Fernão Peres were blamed. The charters show the break: on 31 March 1128 mother and son still signed together, but by 27 April Afonso confirmed the charter of Guimarães without her. Shortly before the battle Theresa and Fernão Peres came into the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> with an army and were defeated near Guimarães. Both were captured, but only expelled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Afonso still had to submit as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of León, and he was at war with Alfonso VII from 1128 to 1137. Theresa died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> two years after the battle, and Fernão Peres became one of Alfonso VII's generals. Afonso cultivated the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> and, after a revolt led by his brother-in-law Bermudo Pérez in 1131, moved his capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guimaraes">Guimarães</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, near the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He declared himself king after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ourique">Battle of Ourique</a> in 1139; León recognised him in the Treaty of Zamora of 1143, and the Holy See in 1179.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of São Mamede” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their son Afonso Henriques defeated his mother’s party at the <b>Battle of São Mamede</b> in 1128 and took control of the county, ruling first from Guimarães and then from Coimbra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Teresa's son Afonso Henriques defeated her party at <b>São Mamede</b> in 1128.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-portugal">County of Portugal</a></li></ul></section>
